        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "d4b940e4-5817-5ff2-904b-a641509f5e9d",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- y z + \\ln{\\left(- x z + y^{2} \\right)}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(2,2,-1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=- \\frac{z}{- x z + y^{2}}\\\\f_y&=\\frac{2 y}{- x z + y^{2}} - z\\\\f_z&=- \\frac{x}{- x z + y^{2}} - y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(2,2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(P)&=- \\frac{\\left(-1\\right)}{- \\left(2\\right) \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(2\\right)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{6}\\\\f_y(P)&=\\frac{2 \\left(2\\right)}{- \\left(2\\right) \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(2\\right)^{2}} - \\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{5}{3}\\\\f_z(P)&=- \\frac{\\left(2\\right)}{- \\left(2\\right) \\left(-1\\right) + \\left(2\\right)^{2}} - \\left(2\\right)=- \\frac{7}{3}\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathbf i+\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\mathbf j-\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-1\\nabla f(P)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathbf i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\mathbf j+\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $-\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\mathbf i-\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\mathbf j+\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$.",
