        "question": {
          ":question/id": "367683a7-e281-5383-b988-4b2f75249518",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\left(x^{2} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\right)^{3}\n$$\n\nfind a nonzero vector perpendicular to the level surface through $P=(0,-2,-1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "At the supplied point, $f(P)=0$. Thus the level surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(x^{2} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\right)^{3}&=0\\\\x^{2} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA real number has cube zero exactly when it is zero, so these equations describe the same surface. By the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f&=3\\left(x^{2} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\\right)^{2}\\nabla g\\\\\\nabla f(P)&=\\mathbf 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe zero vector has no direction. It does not establish that the surface lacks a normal. Use the equivalent defining function\n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^{2} + \\left(y + 1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(z + 1\\right)^{2} - 1\n$$\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x&=2 x\\\\g_y&=2 y + 2\\\\g_z&=4 z + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(0,-2,-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng_x(P)&=2 \\left(0\\right)=0\\\\g_y(P)&=2 \\left(-2\\right) + 2=-2\\\\g_z(P)&=4 \\left(-1\\right) + 4=0\\\\\\nabla g(P)&=-2\\,\\mathbf j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla g(P)=4\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf j$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$.",
