          ":question/id": "76de4e00-c400-5d0b-9564-d2ce8f815d23",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\n- 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + y z + z^{2}=2 z + 6\n$$\n\nat $P=(-1,0,-2)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Move all terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + y z + z^{2} - 2 z - 6\n$$\n\nThe point satisfies $F(P)=0$. Include derivatives of the terms moved from the right side.\n\nA nonzero gradient is perpendicular to its level surface. Treat the other variables as constants when taking each partial derivative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x&=- 4 x\\\\F_y&=4 y + z\\\\F_z&=y + 2 z - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P=(-1,0,-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(P)&=- 4 \\left(-1\\right)=4\\\\F_y(P)&=4 \\left(0\\right) + \\left(-2\\right)=-2\\\\F_z(P)&=\\left(0\\right) + 2 \\left(-2\\right) - 2=-6\\\\\\nabla F(P)&=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero.\n\nEvery nonzero scalar multiple of this gradient is normal. In particular,\n\n$$\n1\\nabla F(P)=4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nSo the listed vector $4\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf j-6\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the surface at $P$.",
